// A block assembled in the context and the message's end, as functions of
// the context (FIPS 180-4 §5.1.1, issues/sha256-context-type.md,
// docs/layout-design.md §9.5 item 4): a byte into the block, the block run
// when the sixty-fourth lands, and zeros to the block's end; the padding
// and the length are `sha256_padding.hthr`, a module of their own so that
// neither's elaboration is both
// (issues/runtime-tests-cold-build-past-rail.md). Each is a `mut self`
// method of `Sha256` with the generator's
// record theorem, and each store into the context is one of these or of
// the compression's (`sha256_compression.hthr`).
//
// A block is assembled in the schedule's first sixteen words, where the
// compression wants it, and a block's words are their bytes with the most
// significant first. So a byte of the block is a quarter of a word:
// `Sha256::put` reads the word, replaces the byte (`u32_be_with_byte`, by
// the byte's place in its word, compiled code having no shift by a word)
// and stores the word. That is three steps where a store of one byte
// would do; a byte of a word array as a place of its own is not built
// (issues/runtime-record-function-limits.md).
//
// What the `runtime` generator reads (issues/runtime-generator-imports.md).
import function_ir_lower;
import function_ir_lower_calls;
